<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one, and let I be its distinct prime divisors. For each subset S, multiply its chosen prime roots to obtain zeta sub S. The empty subset gives one. At that input, let r sub S be d divided by c, and w sub S be g divided by c.<break time="600ms"/>Use one ring A: the integers with i, the chosen roots, and one over u adjoined. The norm identities make every evaluated polynomial a unit in A. In particular, the inverse of c at zeta sub S is its complex conjugate divided by u squared. Thus all these ratios belong to the same ring and are units.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Node six gives an exact alternating product of the r ratios equal to one. An even subset contributes its ratio with exponent positive one. An odd subset contributes the inverse, with exponent negative one. Move the even factors above a fraction bar and the odd factors below it. This is the same exact product.<break time="600ms"/>Now multiply both sides by the odd-subset product. It is a product of units. The denominator cancels, leaving the even-subset product equal to the odd-subset product. This equality holds in A, before reduction. All factors now have positive exponents.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, there are just two subsets: empty, and the subset containing three. The product equality is simply r at the empty subset equal to r at the subset containing three.<break time="600ms"/>For u equal to fifteen, the two distinct primes are three and five. There are four subsets. The even subsets are empty and the pair three, five; the odd subsets are the two singletons. The exact equality is r at the empty subset times r at the pair, equal to r at three times r at five. This constrains two products. It does not require all four individual ratios to coincide. Keep the product identity for the general argument.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>The w ratios obey the same exact alternating-product identity. Move their odd-subset factors across in the same way. Their even-subset product equals their odd-subset product, again in A. Keep both equalities together.<break time="600ms"/>We now have finite products with only positive exponents, built from the same evaluation ring. The next step can expand each side directly and compare the linear terms.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
